\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces {\bf  Calibration of two synaptic models, $\alpha $ and logistic, and validation of inhibitory coupling mechanisms. Fact-check list IV:} Compare panels\nobreakspace  {}C and\nobreakspace  {}D with the corresponding neurophysiological recordings presented in Fig.\nobreakspace  {}7 of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. (A–B) Three positive current pulses of progressively increasing amplitude were applied to Si2R to calibrate the $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse models (bottom trace), as well as to reproduce the inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) responses observed in the contralateral Si2L (top trace in panel\nobreakspace  {}B). (C–D) Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R evoked accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top trace) and elicited both fast and slow echo responses in Si3L. These echoes arise from distinct mechanisms: a fast component mediated by the electrical coupling and a slower, delayed inhibitory response mediated by the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L. Together, these features reproduce the experimentally observed dynamics and confirm the functional roles of electrical and chemical inhibition within the swim CPG circuitry (see Fig.\nobreakspace  {}2A).}}{15}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{7}{15}{{\bf Calibration of two synaptic models, $\alpha $ and logistic, and validation of inhibitory coupling mechanisms. Fact-check list IV:} Compare panels~C and~D with the corresponding neurophysiological recordings presented in Fig.~7 of Ref.~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}. (A–B) Three positive current pulses of progressively increasing amplitude were applied to Si2R to calibrate the $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse models (bottom trace), as well as to reproduce the inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) responses observed in the contralateral Si2L (top trace in panel~B). (C–D) Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R evoked accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top trace) and elicited both fast and slow echo responses in Si3L. These echoes arise from distinct mechanisms: a fast component mediated by the electrical coupling and a slower, delayed inhibitory response mediated by the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L. Together, these features reproduce the experimentally observed dynamics and confirm the functional roles of electrical and chemical inhibition within the swim CPG circuitry (see Fig.~2A)}{figure.7}{}}
